## Why This Is Asked
Models practical scheduling under capacity with a convex (squared) day cost, exercising 2D DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and days
- Transition constrained by daily capacity D
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Backtracking via parent pointers to recover daily blocks
- Complexity: O(n^2 m) time, O(n m) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSchedule(A, m, D) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(m + 1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(m + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let d = 1; d <= m; d++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const sum = pref[i] - pref[j];
        if (sum <= D && dp[j][d - 1] !== INF) {
          const cost = dp[j][d - 1] + sum * sum;
          if (cost < dp[i][d]) {
            dp[i][d] = cost;
            par[i][d] = j;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  if (dp[n][m] === INF) return { cost: -1, blocks: [] };
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, d = m;
  while (d > 0) {
    const j = par[i][d];
    blocks.push([j + 1, i]);
    i = j;
    d--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { cost: dp[n][m], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to near O(n m) if D is small or sums have tighter bounds?
- How would you present the actual day blocks to the user, including start/end indices and sums?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates dynamic programming expertise across string partitioning with fixed segment counts, numeric parsing constraints, and solution reconstruction—fundamental skills for real-world interview scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over string prefixes and partition counts
- Handling substring numeric values with leading-zero validation
- Solution path reconstruction for determining optimal cut positions
- Time and space complexity optimization techniques

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;

  // Helper to get numeric value of substring [l, r)
  const getValue = (l, r) => {
    if (s[l] === '0' && r - l > 1) return null; // leading zero
    return Number(s.slice(l, r));
  };

  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let j = t - 1; j < i; j++) {
        const val = getValue(j, i);
        if (val !== null && dp[j][t-1] + val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[j][t-1] + val;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0) {
    const prev = parent[curr][currT];
    cuts.push(prev);
    curr = prev;
    currT--;
  }
  cuts.pop(); // remove 0
  cuts.reverse();

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Notes

- The DP explores all possible cut positions while respecting the k-substring constraint
- Leading zero validation is crucial for correctness
- The parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal cuts

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model budgeted multi-interval optimization where a fixed daily cost incentivizes shorter campaigns. Requires stateful DP with reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- Up to k non-overlapping subarrays
- Per-element cost transformation: B[i] = A[i] - c
- DP states: end[i][t], best[i][t]
- Recurrences: end[i][t] = max(end[i-1][t] + B[i], best[i-1][t-1] + B[i]); best[i][t] = max(best[i-1][t], end[i][t])
- Reconstruction via backpointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarraysWithCost(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const B = A.map(x => x - c);
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const best = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prevEnd = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(null));

  best[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    end[i][0] = -Infinity;
    best[i][0] = 0;
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const extend = end[i-1][t] + B[i-1];
      const startNew = best[i-1][t-1] + B[i-1];
      end[i][t] = Math.max(extend, startNew);
      if (best[i-1][t] >= end[i][t]) {
        best[i][t] = best[i-1][t];
      } else {
        best[i][t] = end[i][t];
        prevEnd[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }

  let bestVal = -Infinity, bestT = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    if (best[n][t] > bestVal) { bestVal = best[n][t]; bestT = t; }
  }
  return {profit: bestVal, segments: []};
}
```

