## Why This Is Asked
Tests a nontrivial DP for partitioning with a per-block cost derived from a range statistic, plus reconstruction across k blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute cost[l][r] = max(A[l..r])*(r-l+1) in O(n^2).
- DP: dp[i][t] = max_{l≤i} dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i].
- Track par[i][t] for block endpoints; base dp[i][1] = cost[0][i].
- Space/time tradeoffs; discuss optimizations (rolling arrays, divide-and-conquer). 

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxBlockPartition(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let m = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      m = Math.max(m, a[r]);
      cost[l][r] = m * (r - l + 1);
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        const val = dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {dp, par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement a faster optimization (e.g., divide-and-conquer) if opt indices are monotone?
- How would you adapt to allow at most k blocks or variable block values (e.g., using different per-block metrics)?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests mastery of DAG dynamic programming, vertex-disjoint path packing, and careful state design to enforce disjointness while meeting exact cardinality constraints.

## Key Concepts
- DAG topological ordering enables DP by ensuring all dependencies are processed first
- Vertex-disjoint paths are guaranteed by tracking endpoint-specific states
- Path reconstruction requires storing predecessor pointers during computation
- Complexity: O(Mk + Nk) time, O(Nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode
const topo = topologicalSort(dag);
const dp = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
const pred = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

for (const v of topo) {
  dp[v][0] = 0;
  const bestPrev = Math.max(...dp[v]);

  for (const u of dag.inEdges(v)) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (dp[u][t] > -Infinity) {
        // Extend existing path
        if (dp[u][t] + val[v] > dp[v][t]) {
          dp[v][t] = dp[u][t] + val[v];
          pred[v][t] = u;
        }
        // Start new path
        if (t < k && bestPrev + val[v] > dp[v][t + 1]) {
          dp[v][t + 1] = bestPrev + val[v];
          pred[v][t + 1] = u;
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

const answer = Math.max(...dp.map(v => v[k]));
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over subsequences with a fixed length and pairwise costs, plus explicit reconstruction. It moves beyond contiguous partitions by requiring last-step linkage through prior picks.

## Key Concepts
- Subsequence DP with fixed length
- Min-over-predecessor recurrence
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and practical optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function solve(A, m){
  const n=A.length;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(m+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1]=0;
  for(let t=2;t<=m;t++){
    for(let i=t-1;i<n;i++){
      for(let j=t-2;j<i;j++){
        const c=dp[j][t-1]+Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if(c<dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=c; par[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best=Infinity,bestI=-1;
  for(let i=m-1;i<n;i++){ if(dp[i][m]<best){ best=dp[i][m]; bestI=i; } }
  const path=[];
  let cur=bestI, t=m;
  while(t>=1){
    path.push(cur);
    cur=par[cur][t];
    t--;
  }
  path.reverse();
  return {cost:best, indices:path};
}
```

## Why This Problem Matters
This problem assesses sophisticated dynamic programming techniques by combining palindrome preprocessing, optimal substructure for non-overlapping segment selection, and explicit solution reconstruction. It integrates preprocessing with suffix-based DP while requiring careful index management.

## Key Concepts
- Palindrome table (isPal) DP preprocessing for O(1) palindrome queries
- 2D DP with optimal substructure and complete solution reconstruction
- Complexity analysis: Time O(n²k), Space O(n² + nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPalBlocks(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const isPal = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(false));

  // Precompute palindrome table
  for (let len = 1; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let l = 0; l + len <= n; l++) {
      const r = l + len - 1;
      isPal[l][r] = (s[l] === s[r] && (len <= 2 || isPal[l+1][r-1]));
    }
  }

  // Initialize DP tables
  const dp = Array.from({length: n + 1}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
  const trace = Array.from({length: n + 1}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  // Fill DP table
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i + 1][t];
      trace[i][t] = {type: 'skip'};

      for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
        if (isPal[i][j]) {
          const val = (j - i + 1) + dp[j + 1][t - 1];
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            trace[i][t] = {type: 'take', l: i, r: j};
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct solution
  const blocks = [];
  let i = 0, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i < n) {
    const choice = trace[i][t];
    if (choice.type === 'take') {
      blocks.push([choice.l, choice.r]);
      i = choice.r + 1;
      t--;
    } else {
      i++;
    }
  }

  return {maxLength: dp[0][k], blocks};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design with an extra edit dimension on a classic string problem; requires careful transitions for matches, edits, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings (i, j)
- Extra dimension for edits (t)
- Transitions for match/mismatch with edits
- Path reconstruction and complexity trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxLPSWithEdits(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) dp[i][i][t] = 1;
  for (let len=2; len<=n; len++) {
    for (let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++) {
      const j = i+len-1;
      for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
        let best = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t]);
        if (s[i] === s[j]) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t] + 2);
        if (t >= 1) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-1] + 2);
        if (t >= 2) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-2] + 2);
        dp[i][j][t] = best;
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k];
}
```

